# THE NAMEPLATE AMOUNTS WHOSE UNIT CARRIES A PERIOD. One row per layer whose supply is quoted
# as a RATE -- `4 GPU-hours per week` -- as against a STOCK, `12 people`.
#
# ⭐⭐ WHY THE DISTINCTION IS A RELATION AND NOT AN `IF`. "What fraction of the period is this
#    supply available?" is a malformed question against a stock: `12 people` has no period, so
#    "five days" has nothing to be five days of. Against a rate it is perfectly answerable, and
#    `notApplicable` there is a refusal dressed as a category error. The set of units that have
#    a period is therefore the thing that decides whether an absence is honest.
#
# ⛔⭐⭐ A DENOMINATOR IS NOT A PERIOD, AND THE NAME OF THIS FILE IS THE PREDICATE IT TESTS.
#    `LIKE '% per %' OR LIKE '% por %'` finds a denominator; this rule needs a PERIOD, and the
#    corpus separates them -- `asrt:factor` files `GPU-hour per GPU`, whose denominator is a
#    GPU. At nameplate grain the two agree on every filing here, so a relation can carry the
#    wrong predicate and the right rows until somebody files the case that splits them.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE TWO EDITIONS STAY OUT OF THE SQL ENTIRELY. `shifts per week` and `turnos por
#    semana` both file `pm:denominator/pm:period`, so nothing here decides that `semana` IS
#    `week`. Written as an `OR` over two spellings that judgement belongs to no filing.
#
# ⛔⛔ `owns` IS A TWO-SEGMENT PATH AND NOT AN ELEMENT NAME, WHICH IS WHAT KEEPS THIS ONE ROW
#    PER LAYER. `Demand/amount` and `Nameplate/amount` are the same element NAME, so a
#    name-keyed `owns` makes this relation answer for the demand too and return TWO ROWS PER
#    LAYER. A semi-join consumer cannot see the duplicate; a fault of that shape sits here
#    undetected until something JOINS on it. A duty cycle is a fraction of the NAMEPLATE's
#    period, and the demand's period is a different fact belonging to a different question.
#    ⛔ AND THE DISAGREEING LAYER ALREADY EXISTS, so this is live rather than latent.
#    `every-absence/delivery` quotes its DEMAND `per day` and files no nameplate amount at
#    all, so a name-keyed predicate admits it on the strength of a fact about demand. It costs
#    nothing only because that layer's window is `unmeasured`, and
#    `window_not_applicable_on_a_rate` examines only `notApplicable` windows. One filing away
#    from a wrong verdict is not the same as safe.
-- pm:Claim/pm:denominator/pm:period, at pm:Nameplate/amount.
SELECT c.filing, c.layer, c.unit, c.denominator AS period
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/claims.sqlc)
) c
WHERE c.owns = 'pm:nameplate/pm:amount'
  AND c.denominator_kind = 'period'
